          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "You should seek care regarding your HbA1c level of 7.2%.\n\nAn HbA1c level of 7.2% means your average blood sugar level has been above the target range, indicating that you likely fall into the category of diagnosed type 2 diabetes, as levels of 6.5% or higher are used for diagnosis (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). The general guideline recommends that many non-pregnant adults with type 2 diabetes maintain an HbA1c level below 7% to reduce the risk of complications such as issues affecting the eyes, kidneys, and nerves (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). \n\nSome individuals may aim for a more stringent goal of below 6.5% if it can be achieved safely, but this is based on specific circumstances (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). \n\nFor coding purposes, type 2 diabetes mellitus is recorded under the ICD-10 code **E11** (source: coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
